    "q-150767": {
      "dom_id": "step-q150767",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131900/assets/de5601836d150d370b9ab7b8d0dceb76bcd0174f77f930f01fb488b772fcbcc6.png)\n\nThe measure of the angle $∠BAD$ is ${100}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠CAD.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠CAD$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAC).$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx={100}^{∘}-{70}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
