    "q-150699": {
      "dom_id": "step-q150699",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131900/assets/b884ae8109d723e9277daa7b076d8a4ff52c07c8295e83d360a07bbb7785dd1d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m∠BAD={90}^{∘}.$ What equation could be used to find the value of $x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠BAC.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠BAC$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠CAD).$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation to determine $x$ is the following:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}-{40}^{∘}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
