    "q-154877": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154877",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14131900/assets/e3fe548e2dd695d682dec13287307e58fe28aa8a01b3e672695ef8ed07c61e1d.png)\n\nThe measure of the angle $∠BAE$ is ${150}^{∘}.$ Find the value of $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠DAE.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAD$ and $∠DAE$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AD}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAE)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAD$ and $∠DAE).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠DAE$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAE)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠BAD).$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={150}^{∘}-{110}^{∘}={40}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
